Lyrics timeline skill: third-party models and software

This skill's own code is MIT licensed (see the repository LICENSE). It ships no model weights and
no third-party code. When the user agrees, scripts/setup.py downloads the models below and installs
the Python packages below into a private virtual environment under the plugin data folder. They are
not redistributed with the plugin; each stays under its own licence.

Models (downloaded on request, Apache-2.0 code and weights)
  Qwen3-ASR-0.6B and Qwen3-ASR-1.7B   Apache-2.0   https://huggingface.co/Qwen
  Qwen3-ForcedAligner-0.6B            Apache-2.0   https://huggingface.co/Qwen/Qwen3-ForcedAligner-0.6B
  Each repo is pinned to one fixed revision (a Hugging Face commit and a ModelScope revision), and
  every file is checked against the size and sha256 in scripts/model_manifest.json before it is
  kept. A file that is not listed there is not downloaded. The Python packages below are pinned to
  exact versions but pip does not check hashes for them (hash-pinned lock files are future work).

Python packages installed by scripts/setup.py (pinned in requirements.in)
  qwen-asr 0.0.6        Apache-2.0   (installed with --no-deps, see below)
  torch                 BSD-3-Clause
  transformers          Apache-2.0   (pinned by qwen-asr)
  accelerate            Apache-2.0   (pinned by qwen-asr)
  librosa               ISC          (required by qwen-asr)
  nagisa                MIT          (imported by qwen-asr at module level)
  numpy                 BSD-3-Clause
  scipy                 BSD-3-Clause
  soundfile             BSD-3-Clause (its wheels bundle libsndfile, LGPL-2.1)
  pypinyin              MIT
  SudachiPy             Apache-2.0   (Japanese readings for the lyric comparison)
  SudachiDict-core      Apache-2.0   (the dictionary SudachiPy reads; 202 MB on disk)
  plus the packages these pull in (numba, scikit-learn, huggingface_hub and so on), each under
  its own licence.

Packages that qwen-asr declares but this skill does not install
  qwen-asr 0.0.6 lists soynlp (GPL-3.0), gradio, flask, pytz, sox and qwen-omni-utils as
  requirements. The wheel source was checked: soynlp is imported only inside the Korean branch of
  the aligner's encode_timestamp (qwen_asr/inference/qwen3_forced_aligner.py, line 243), gradio only
  by the demo command (qwen_asr/cli/demo.py, line 27), flask only by the streaming demo command
  (qwen_asr/cli/demo_streaming.py, line 34), and pytz, sox and qwen-omni-utils are not imported at
  all. So scripts/setup.py installs qwen-asr with --no-deps and the explicit list above, which
  leaves out soynlp, gradio, flask, pytz, sox and qwen-omni-utils. nagisa is kept: qwen-asr imports
  it at module level (line 21 of the same aligner file), and it is the aligner's Japanese word
  splitter. Korean lyrics are sent to the aligner under a language name that selects its space
  splitter (one unit per word), so the soynlp branch is never reached. The qwen-asr demo commands
  do not work without their packages; they are not used here.

Excluded on purpose (licence)
  MMS models and ctc-forced-aligner defaults (CC-BY-NC-4.0), SOFA / HubertFA / SingAlign weights
  (non-commercial), whisper-timestamped (AGPL), SongPrep (academic use only), SongFormer and its
  MuQ dependency (non-commercial), TEN VAD (extra terms; this skill uses an energy and pitch gate).

Test-only dependencies (requirements-test.txt): pytest (MIT), numpy, pypinyin, SudachiPy,
SudachiDict-core.
